// ⚠ RE-DERIVED: THE ALIAS THIS FILE WAS BUILT TO PIN NO LONGER EXISTS.
//
// It was the false-negative sensor for mono's CONCRETE bare-spelling alias.
// That alias is now gone — both of mono's bare aliases are — and this file was
// kept and re-derived rather than deleted, because the program it constructs
// still pins a real invariant and a sharper one:
//
//   a HASH CONTAINER must instantiate in a program that links an archive
//   declaring its own homonym `trait Hash`. `HashMap<i64,i64>::insert` reaches
//   mono only through the method worklist, and its bound must be satisfied by
//   the STDLIB `Hash` — not refused because a homonym occupies the name, and
//   not admitted through a bare-keyed duplicate of every fact.
//
// That now rests on two things: facts keyed by the always-qualified identity,
// and `Mono::bare_trait_identities_` resolving the compiler's own bare-text
// probes. If this file reds, one of those two is broken — which is the same
// failure the alias used to mask.
//
// HOW THE REMOVAL WAS MEASURED, because THIS alias was once deleted on a green
// suite and that deletion was a real regression. A print inside the guarded
// block reports it firing 475 times while compiling THIS file (452 for
// trait_ident_pkg_chain), so the branch was emphatically live. Removing it then
// left the build complete and the corpus green — L2 1908/1908, L4 clean.
// Reached AND no-op is a measurement. A green suite over a branch nobody proved
// was reached is not, and both of this arc's mistakes live in that gap.
//
// ⚠ THE NOTE BELOW ABOUT "no corpus member links a homonym archive AND uses a
// hash container" IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL — it was written to be that
// member. It still is. Do not delete it on the grounds that the alias is gone.
//
// ── The original header follows; it is the record of how the blind spot was
// ── found, and why this program has the shape it has.
//
// THE BARE-SPELLING ALIAS IN `Mono`'s IMPL INDEX, PINNED BY ITS FALSE-NEGATIVE
// DIRECTION — the one the corpus could not see.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Mono files every concrete impl fact TWICE: once under
// the trait's canonical identity, and once under its BARE spelling as an alias
// (`Mono::Mono`'s impl-indexing loop, `if (impl_canon != impl_trait)`). A round
// on 2026-08-10 measured that the alias does NOT produce the wrong ANSWER it was
// suspected of — a query naming the bare-slot trait does not see a homonym's
// impls — and deleted it as "necessity without a consumer", on a corpus that
// stayed green.
//
// The consumer is on the OTHER SIDE, and green was not evidence about it. The
// bare-text `mono_has_impl_recursive` / `mono_concrete_satisfies_bound` call
// sites do NOT go through `resolve_trait_query_name`: they pass the blanket's
// RAW `bi.bound_trait` text. So removing the alias cannot cause a false
// positive — it causes a false NEGATIVE, a bound that stops being satisfied and
// a method that stops being instantiated.
//
// MEASURED, both arms, full rebuild each:
//   alias ON  → rc 0, this file returns 0
//   alias OFF → `logosc` rc 1:
//     "void call statement DROPPED — the method 'HashMap$G2$i64$i64__insert'
//      had no instantiation, so the whole call was silently discarded
//      (mono never demanded the callee)"
//   and identically for `HashMap<(i64,i64),i64>` through `impl<A: Hash, B: Hash>
//   Hash for (A,B)`.
//
// ⚠ AND THE SHAPE IS THE POINT: no OTHER corpus member links a homonym archive
// AND uses a hash container, so the class was invisible to L4 BY CONSTRUCTION.
// A green suite said nothing about it, which is exactly how the deletion came to
// look safe. That is what this file changes.
//
// ⚠ THIS FIXTURE PINS A HEDGE, NOT A RULE. The alias is a superset that hides a
// real conflation one layer down (`SemaChecker::collect_impl` keys `impls_` as
// `trait_name + "::" + target` from the RAW spelling — one key space for two
// traits). When bound trait names are canonicalised at emit, the alias goes AND
// this fixture must be re-derived against the canonical bound, not deleted:
// what it asserts — that a hash container still instantiates in a program that
// links a homonym archive — must remain true.
//
// The import of `trait_ident_chain.hmid` is load-bearing: it drags in `lhom`,
// whose own `trait Hash` takes the BARE registry slot in this translation unit
// (B-mv-02 gives it to whoever is collected first, and an archive's decls land
// before the stdlib's). Without that import the stdlib `Hash` owns the bare slot
// and the alias is a no-op, so the file would pin nothing.
package test;

use trait_ident_chain.hmid;
use logos.mem.collections.hashmap;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut m: HashMap<i64, i64> = hashmap_new::<i64, i64>();
    unsafe { m.insert(1i64, 2i64); }
    if m.len() != 1i64 { return 1i32; }

    // the tuple key exercises `impl<A: Hash, B: Hash> Hash for (A, B)` — a
    // BLANKET bound over the same bare spelling, the second shape measured.
    let mut t: HashMap<(i64, i64), i64> = hashmap_new::<(i64, i64), i64>();
    unsafe { t.insert((3i64, 4i64), 5i64); }
    if t.len() != 1i64 { return 2i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
